<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="9e465c30">&lt;p&gt;
    Standards outline how policies will be implemented and provide a set of rules to enforce the policies along with
    explicit exception processes where applicable. Corporate level policies will comprise technology independent standards,
    and architectural level policies will call out specific technologies or products. Standards may be driven by any of the
    following:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Legal and Regulatory Obligations:&lt;/strong&gt; These standards are mandated by law and therefore an enterprise
        must comply or face serious consequences.  This includes &quot;tax law&quot; which would cover statutory filings, tax
        returns, tax state charters, etc. 
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Industry Standards:&lt;/strong&gt; These standards are established by industry bodies, such as The Open Group,
        and are then selected by the enterprise for adoption. Industry standards offer potential for interoperation and
        sharing across enterprises, but also fall outside of the control of the enterprise and therefore must be actively
        monitored.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Organizational Standards:&lt;/strong&gt; These standards are set within the organization and are based on
        business aspiration (e.g., selection of standard applications to support portfolio consolidation). 
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Standards may be further broken out into specific types including:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Standard business processes
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Standard role definitions
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Security and privacy standards
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Accessibility standards
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Design standards
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Development standards
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Data standards
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Technology standards (platform, vendor, etc.)
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="25d53d02"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="cfff0280">Standards should ideally represent best practice and be written in sufficient detail as to make it relatively
straightforward to determine compliance.</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="3dd2640a"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="255484d9">&lt;p&gt;
    The purpose of this work product is to:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Add more specificity to policies in order to effectively implement them in a consistent and measurable fashion
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Provide a frame of reference for organizational preferences and specifically identify predetermined design
        decisions
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="impactOfNotHaving" nodeId="85a9025b">&lt;p&gt;
    Even in projects where standards are not explicitly in scope there may be de facto standards that need to be
    considered.  Not having these formally documented leads to potential miscommunications and subsequent rework where
    the implied standards were not met.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    In cases where regulatory standards exist, failure to follow those standards could lead to potential exposures
    that could have been preventable. 
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="reasonsForNotNeeding" nodeId="c89525b0">&lt;p&gt;
    If a set of applicable standards exists those may be adopted instead of creating a new set, however this will typically
    require some level of review to determine compliance with those standards.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
